# SYNOPSIS

```swift
import OpenType     // a font's tables and outlines
import Shape        // text to positioned glyphs
import Raster       // outlines to coverage
import MSDF         // outlines to distance fields
import GlyphAtlas   // the glyph atlas and cache
import TextLayout   // paragraphs set and drawn

struct Font {
  init(_ data: [UInt8]) throws(FontError)
  let glyphCount: Int
  let metrics: Metrics
  func glyph(for scalar: UInt32) -> Int
  func advance(_ glyph: Int) throws(FontError) -> Int
  func outline(_ glyph: Int) throws(FontError) -> Outline
  var familyName: String? { get }
}

struct Shaper {
  init(_ font: Font)
  func shape(_ text: [UInt32], script: String = "latn", language: String? = nil,
             features: [Feature] = []) -> [ShapedGlyph]
}
struct Rasterizer {
  mutating func render(_ outline: Outline, scale: Double,
                       x: Double = 0, y: Double = 0,
                       into bitmap: inout Bitmap)
}

struct DistanceFieldGenerator {
  mutating func generate(_ outline: Outline, scale: Double,
                         x: Double = 0, y: Double = 0,
                         into field: inout DistanceField)
}

struct GlyphCache {
  init(coverageSize: Int = 1024, fieldSize atlas: Int = 1024)
  mutating func coverage(_ font: Font, fontID: UInt32, glyph: Int,
                         pixelsPerEm: Double, quarter: UInt8 = 0)
    -> AtlasRegion?
  mutating func field(_ font: Font, fontID: UInt32, glyph: Int)
    -> AtlasRegion?
}

struct Typesetter {
  init(_ font: Font)
  func lineHeight(_ size: Double) -> Double
  func set(_ text: [UInt32], size: Double, width: Double,
           x: Double = 0, y: Double = 0, features: [Feature] = [],
           tracking: Double = 0) -> Paragraph
}

struct Canvas {
  var contrast: TextContrast   // light(2)
  init(width: Int, height: Int, color: UInt32)
  mutating func fill(x: Int, y: Int, width: Int, height: Int, _ color: UInt32)
}

protocol PixelTarget {   // what a renderer draws on: Canvas, PixelView
  var width: Int { get }
  var height: Int { get }
  var contrast: TextContrast { get set }
  mutating func blend(_ x: Int, _ y: Int, _ color: UInt32, _ coverage: Int)
  mutating func blendText(_ x: Int, _ y: Int, _ color: UInt32, _ coverage: Int,
                          alpha: UInt32)
  mutating func blend(row y: Int, _ x0: Int, _ x1: Int, _ color: UInt32,
                      _ coverage: Int)
  mutating func blendText(rows y: Int, _ count: Int, _ x0: Int, _ x1: Int,
                          coverage: UnsafePointer<UInt8>, stride: Int,
                          ink: inout PixelKernels.TextInk)
}

struct PixelView: PixelTarget {   // pixels drawn in place
  init(_ base: UnsafeMutablePointer<UInt32>, stride: Int,
       x0: Int = 0, y0: Int = 0, width: Int, height: Int)
  mutating func fill(x0: Int, y0: Int, x1: Int, y1: Int, _ color: UInt32)
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

The text stack turns Unicode text and an OpenType font into pixels.
It is six libraries, each over the ones before it, all tier 0 and written from the specifications: none calls the system, so a service, an app or a host tool links them alike.

`OpenType` reads a font from its bytes: its tables, metrics, `cmap` both ways, names, and each glyph's advance and outline (TrueType `glyf` or CFF), unhinted and in font units.
Its metrics carry the face's weight and width from `OS/2`, the width class as a percentage of normal (87 for semi-condensed, 125 for expanded), by which a theme's typefaces choose their faces (uicore(2)).
A font that isn't one, or whose tables don't hold what they say, throws `FontError`.

`Shape` is the default shaper.
`shape` takes Unicode scalars and OpenType tags for the script, the language and the features, and returns glyphs with their clusters, advances and offsets in font units.
It normalizes the text for the font, applies GSUB's lookups and GPOS's (or the `kern` table, or positions marks itself in a font without GPOS), and hides default ignorables.
A second form fills an array the caller keeps, so its storage is reused.

`Raster` turns an outline into a coverage bitmap: the exact signed area under the outline, flattened to 1/128 pixel.
A `Rasterizer` and a `Bitmap` keep their storage; reuse them across glyphs.

`MSDF` makes multi-channel signed distance fields, the true distance in alpha, for text drawn at any size from one field.

`GlyphAtlas` packs coverage bitmaps and fields into two atlases and finds them again by font, glyph, size and quarter-pixel position.
It has no dictionary, but an open-addressing table.
An `Atlas` is as wide as it was made, and begins with no rows: it grows taller as it fills, doubling from 32 rows, up to the height it was made with, which is `maxHeight`; `height` is the rows it has now.
What it placed keeps its place as it grows, and a renderer that uploads it makes its texture again when `height` changes.
Full at `maxHeight`, it clears and begins a new generation, so a renderer finds its glyphs again each frame.
`takeDirty` gives the rectangle changed since it was last taken, for an upload.

`TextLayout` sets a paragraph: it shapes the text, breaks it into lines where UAX #14 allows (TDUnicode's segmentation) and never past the width but for a word that can't be broken, and places each glyph.
`Canvas` holds 0x00RRGGBB pixels, sRGB, which a renderer (uicore(2)) draws into, blending in linear light (light(2)) after each glyph's coverage takes the canvas's `contrast` curve, the theme's `text.contrast`.
`PixelView` is memory someone else owns, a window's buffer, drawn on as a `Canvas` is: its pixel (`x0`, `y0`) is the first of `base`, rows `stride` pixels apart, and nothing lands outside [`x0`, `width`) by [`y0`, `height`).
A `PixelTarget` blends a pixel at a time (`blend`, `blendText`), or along a row from `x0` to `x1` (`blend(row:)`) through pixelkernels(2), the same bytes as each pixel's call; text goes `count` rows from `y` at a time (`blendText(rows:)`), a glyph in one call, its coverage a byte a pixel and its rows `stride` bytes apart, in an ink whose colour, alpha and contrast the caller sets (pixelkernels(2)'s `TextInk`). `Canvas.fill` fills its rows through pixelkernels(2).

Fonts are data, not code: each face in `data/fonts` comes with its licence and a PROVENANCE.md naming its source, commit and checksums.
The host's FreeType and HarfBuzz are the tests' oracles, linked by the host tests only and never into anything on the device: the reader's outlines and metrics agree with FreeType's on the test fonts, and the shaper's glyphs, clusters and positions with HarfBuzz's on English, the European alphabets, Vietnamese, Greek and Cyrillic.
`TODHCHAI_FONT_CORPUS=1` compares every font under `/usr/share/fonts` as well.

